  {topic:'distance-cost',title:'A shorter shot now leaves a longer shot next',text:`Laying back means choosing a shorter shot to avoid trouble. But the next shot will be longer. In the professional golf data we use, leaving 150 yd instead of 100 yd adds about ${(baselineStrokes('fairway',150)-baselineStrokes('fairway',100)).toFixed(2)} strokes to finish the hole. That is ${((baselineStrokes('fairway',150)-baselineStrokes('fairway',100))*100).toFixed(1)} per 100 similar shots. A safer tee shot can be worth it—just check what it leaves you next. These averages are not a prediction of your own score.`},
  {topic:'distance-increments',title:'Not every extra 50 yards costs the same',text:`An extra 50 yards does not always add the same difficulty. In these fairway examples, 50→100 yd adds ${(baselineStrokes('fairway',100)-baselineStrokes('fairway',50)).toFixed(2)} strokes; 100→150 adds ${(baselineStrokes('fairway',150)-baselineStrokes('fairway',100)).toFixed(2)}. Those are almost the same. But 150→200 adds ${(baselineStrokes('fairway',200)-baselineStrokes('fairway',150)+1e-9).toFixed(2)}, a bigger jump. Longer clubs often make the next shot harder. In the next example, both tee targets are reachable. We’ll compare the safer 190 yd shot with a longer shot that leaves you closer to the green.`}
